// Example sheet: keys that work in bash and zsh (emacs mode, the default), and a few
// commands. Make it yours: delete what you know, add what you keep forgetting.
# Shell: keys
Ctrl-R                  search your command history (again: older matches)
Ctrl-A / Ctrl-E         jump to the start / end of the line
Alt-B / Alt-F           move one word back / forward
Ctrl-U                  delete from the cursor to the start of the line
Ctrl-K                  delete from the cursor to the end of the line
Ctrl-W                  delete the word before the cursor
Ctrl-Y                  paste what you just deleted
Alt-.                   insert the last argument of the previous command
Ctrl-L                  clear the screen
Ctrl-X Ctrl-E           edit the current command in $EDITOR
Ctrl-C / Ctrl-Z         stop / pause the running command (fg resumes it)

# Shell: commands
$ cd -                  back to the previous folder
!!                      the previous command (sudo !! runs it again as root)
$ history | grep WORD   find a command you ran before
$ fg                    resume the paused command
$ jobs                  commands paused or running in the background
$ du -sh * | sort -h    what takes space in this folder
$ df -h                 free space on each disk
$ chmod +x FILE         make a script executable
